-Therefore vanishing shell sum along any deterministic subsequence n_j->infinity suffices to exclude every bigeodesic; the stronger rate sup_(x in S_n)q(x)=o(1/n) suffices as well. The published q(x)->0 has no stated rate that yields either premise. Its sublinear expected number of tree exit points is compatible with a constant number of exit points caused by a two-ended path. The single hardest remaining piece for this route is proving vanishing shell incidence, not proving compactness or invoking sublinearity again.
+The incidence inequality is correct, but its proposed vanishing right-hand side is unattainable. Every infinite simple ray from 0 hits S_n, so sum_(x in S_n)q(x)>=P(T_0 nonempty)=1 in the standard iid setting. Thus the shell maximum is at least 1/(8n), and neither a vanishing shell sum nor an o(1/n) maximum can hold. Retain this inequality as an audit scar; use genuinely two-sided extension witnesses or full boundary-geodesic occupation for the unresolved closing estimate.
